    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "\"In 1962, Congress authorized the Continental Margin Program, a joint program between the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) and the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution (WHOI) to conduct a geological reconnaissance investigation of the continental shelf and slope off the Atlantic coast of the United States (Emery and Schlee, 1963). As part of this program 464 photographs were collected at 378 stations from the Canadian border to the southern tip of Florida (Figure 1). Bottom photography was conducted at many of these stations in conjunction with sediment sampling for grain size, mineralogy, geochemistry, and biology. The purpose of this report is to release digital versions of these bottom photographs in a gallery that can be visualized geographically. Information from these photographs is also being merged with data from other federal agencies as part of the Aggregates Project of the USGS Coastal and Marine Geology Program.\"",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
